      "ref": "[D519]",
      "area": "contact",
      "claim": "A parent reports that strangers can message or voice chat with your child in Minecraft.",
      "stance": "risk",
      "quote": "Online multiplayer games allow kids to chat with friends *and* strangers. And while most of these players may be harmless, some have far more dangerous intentions."
    },
    {
      "ref": "[D519]",
      "area": "money",
      "claim": "The game pushes in-app purchases that can be targeted by scammers to steal payment details.",
      "stance": "risk",
      "quote": "Gaming platforms have created their own economies using virtual currencies like V-Bucks (Fortnite), Robux (Roblox), and Minecoins (Minecraft). While these might seem like play money to kids, they have actual cash value and attract sophisticated criminals."
    },
    {
      "ref": "[D519]",
      "area": "data",
      "claim": "The game collects personal information and payment data that can be shared or stolen.",
      "stance": "risk",
      "quote": "Modern gaming accounts often collect valuable personal information, saved payment methods, and digital items worth real money. This makes them attractive targets for cybercriminals, leading to financial loss, identity theft, and danger for young gamers."
    },
    {
      "ref": "[D519]",
      "area": "age_gating",
      "claim": "A parent reports that the age gate does not reliably block younger children from accessing the game.",
      "stance": "neutral",
      "quote": "The latest [Common Sense Census: Media Use by Kids Zero to Eight](/sites/default/files/research/report/2025-common-sense-census-web-2.pdf) shows that gaming time has increased by 65% since 2020, with children age 8 and under now playing for an average of 38 minutes per day."
    },
    {
